{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-7282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=9,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-7282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =9(1)+C \\\\ C & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=9t-9.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-7282" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0"} {"question" "q-7282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-7282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=0"] ["math" "x(t)=9"] ["math" "x(t)=9t"] ["math" "x(t)=9t+1"] ["math" "x(t)=9t-9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-7282" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=9t-9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=3$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0))+C \\\\ 3 & =0+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=3+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "x(t)=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "x(t)=3-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102782" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=3+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(1+1)^{2}}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t+1}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{2}{1+1}+K \\\\ 1 & =1+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}-1"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{16}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{16}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =0+C \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))"] ["math" "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-351552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6{t}^{2}+8,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=15$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-351552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+8t+C \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}+8t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=15.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =2(1)^{3}+8(1)+C \\\\ 15 & =2+8+C \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{3}+8t+5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-351552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0"} {"question" "q-351552" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"} {"question" "q-351552" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2t^3+8t+5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "3a5ecad87f2a4e10fe4d807f3473997fd2fc1b1181aec4f72d1ff2475a9d4e4b"}] "usage" {"q-7282" []} "basis" 1281 "retractions" [[17592186064860 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 9$, where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x = 0$ when $t = 1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t > 0$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063670 17592186063671 17592186063672 17592186063673 17592186063674] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063669 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063669 :answer-field/id] [17592186063669 :answer-field/key] [17592186063669 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
